      </div>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800">
        <div class="text-xs text-slate-400">Decision Window</div>
        <div class="text-xl font-bold text-blue-400 font-mono mt-1">20 Days</div>
        <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400">Positive silence rule</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Content Body -->
    <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none space-y-10 text-slate-300 text-sm leading-relaxed">
      <!-- Section 1 -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">1. The 2026 Statutory Income Threshold Calculation</h2>
        <p>
          Under Spain's <em>Ley de Fomento del Ecosistema de las Empresas Emergentes</em> (widely known as the Startup Act or <em>Ley de Startups</em>), the minimum financial requirement for international remote workers (<em>teletrabajadores de carácter internacional</em>) is tied directly to Spain's statutory minimum wage: the <strong>Salario Mínimo Interprofesional (SMI)</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The law establishes that a solo applicant must demonstrate recurrent monthly earnings equal to or exceeding <strong>200% of the SMI</strong>. Because Spanish employment law distributes the SMI over 14 annualized payments (12 monthly wages plus 2 extra payments in summer and December), the official calculation is codified as follows:
        </p>

        <!-- Income Calculation Table -->
        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-xs sm:text-sm border border-slate-800 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
            <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-white font-semibold">
              <tr>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-slate-800">Applicant Composition</th>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-slate-800">% of SMI</th>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-slate-800">Monthly Requirement (EUR)</th>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-slate-800">Annualized Requirement (EUR)</th>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-slate-800">Approx USD Equivalent</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800 bg-slate-950/60 font-mono">
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-sans font-semibold text-white">Solo Nomad</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-amber-400">200% SMI</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-amber-400 font-bold">€2,646 / mo</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-300">€31,752 / yr</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-400">~$2,890 / mo</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-sans font-semibold text-white">Primary + Spouse (or Partner)</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-300">275% SMI</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-white font-bold">€3,638 / mo</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-300">€43,656 / yr</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-400">~$3,975 / mo</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-sans font-semibold text-white">Family of 3 (Spouse + 1 Child)</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-300">300% SMI</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-white font-bold">€3,969 / mo</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-300">€47,628 / yr</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-400">~$4,335 / mo</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-sans font-semibold text-white">Each Additional Dependent Child</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-400">+25% SMI</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-emerald-400">+€331 / mo</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-400">+€3,969 / yr</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-400">+~$360 / mo</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-amber-500/20 text-xs text-slate-300">
          <strong class="text-amber-400">Proof Protocol:</strong> The UGE-CE requires certified bank statements showing monthly deposits for the past 3 consecutive months, supported by an official pay slip or invoicing ledger, plus an authorized letter from your employer or clients certifying continuous remote engagement.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2 -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">2. The Modified Beckham Law (24% Flat Expat Tax)</h2>
        <p>
          One of the greatest financial incentives of Spain's Digital Nomad Visa is access to the <strong>Special Expatriate Tax Regime (Regimen Especial para Trabajadores Desplazados)</strong>, colloquially known as the <em>Beckham Law</em> (Article 93 of the Spanish Personal Income Tax Act - LIRPF).
        </p>
        <p>
          Prior to the Startup Act, the Beckham Law was virtually restricted to high-ranking corporate executives transferred to Spanish corporate entities. The reform explicitly opened the regime to remote employees and independent contractors holding the Digital Nomad Visa.
        </p>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-6">
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white mb-2">Standard Spanish Resident Tax</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-xs text-slate-400">
              <li>• Progressive brackets climbing from 19% up to 47% (or 50% in certain autonomous regions like Catalonia).</li>
              <li>• Worldwide income taxed: global dividends, rental income, and stock sales subject to Spanish tax.</li>
              <li>• Subject to Modelo 720 (Declaration of foreign assets over €50,000) and Spanish Wealth Tax (Impuesto sobre el Patrimonio).</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-emerald-500/30">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-emerald-400 mb-2">Beckham Law Expat Regime</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-xs text-slate-300">
              <li>• <strong>Flat 24% tax rate</strong> on all earned Spanish income up to €600,000 (47% on excess).</li>
              <li>• <strong>0% tax on non-Spanish investment income:</strong> Foreign capital gains, interest, and dividends outside Spain are completely non-taxable.</li>
              <li>• <strong>Exemption from Modelo 720:</strong> No requirement to disclose foreign bank accounts or assets.</li>
              <li>• Valid for the year of arrival plus the next 5 consecutive fiscal years (6 tax years total).</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="text-xs text-slate-400">
          <em>Important Filing Notice:</em> You must apply for the Beckham Law within six months of registering with Spanish Social Security or starting your qualifying economic activity by filing <strong>Modelo 149</strong> with the Agencia Tributaria.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">3. Remote Work Eligibility: Employees vs Freelancers</h2>
        <p>
          Spain strictly segregates applicants into two distinct categories, each carrying specific evidentiary and social security requirements:
        </p>

        <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/90 border border-slate-800">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
              <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white">A. Remote Employees (Cuenta Ajena)</h3>
              <span class="text-xs font-mono text-amber-400 bg-amber-500/10 px-2 py-0.5 rounded border border-amber-500/20">W-2 / Contracted</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 mb-2">
              Must prove direct employment with a company established outside of Spain for at least 3 months prior to application, with company operations active for at least 1 year.
            </p>
            <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800/80 text-xs text-slate-400">
              <strong class="text-slate-200">Social Security Caveat:</strong> The employer must grant written permission for full-time telework in Spain and either provide a Certificate of Coverage under a bilateral Social Security convention or register with Spanish Social Security as a foreign employer.
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/90 border border-slate-800">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
              <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white">B. Independent Freelancers (Cuenta Propia / Autónomo)</h3>
              <span class="text-xs font-mono text-emerald-400 bg-emerald-500/10 px-2 py-0.5 rounded border border-emerald-500/20">1099 / Contractors</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 mb-2">
              Self-employed professionals must demonstrate ongoing contractual service agreements with one or more clients located outside of Spain.
            </p>
            <div class="p-3 rounded-lg bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800/80 text-xs text-slate-400">
              <strong class="text-emerald-400">The 80/20 Rule:</strong> Under Article 74 bis of the law, freelance digital nomads may earn income from clients located inside Spain, provided that Spanish client billing does not exceed <strong>20% of their total gross revenue</strong>.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4 -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">4. Consular Filing vs In-Country Fast Track (UGE-CE)</h2>
        <p>
          One of the unique advantages of Spain's legislation is the choice between applying via your home Spanish consulate or applying directly while physically in Spain:
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-xs sm:text-sm border border-slate-800 rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
            <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-white font-semibold">
              <tr>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-slate-800">Metric</th>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-slate-800">Consular Submission (Home Country)</th>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-slate-800 text-amber-400">In-Country Submission (UGE-CE)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800 bg-slate-950/60">
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-white">Document Granted</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-400">1-Year Visa sticker in passport</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">3-Year Residence Permit (TIE Card)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-white">Where to Submit</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-400">Spanish Embassy / BLS Center abroad</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-white">Online via Mercurio / UGE portal while in Spain on 90-day tourist status</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-white">Processing Time</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-slate-400">4 to 12 weeks (variable by consulate)</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-amber-400 font-bold">20 Business Days statutory limit</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-white">Silence Rule</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-red-400">Negative silence (deemed rejected)</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">Positive silence (deemed approved)</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>
          <strong>Why In-Country Filing Is Recommended:</strong> The <em>silencio administrativo positivo</em> guarantees that if the UGE-CE does not issue a formal resolution or formal request for further evidence (<em>requerimiento</em>) within 20 business days from file submission, the application is legally approved by default under Spanish administrative procedure.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5 -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">5. Statutory Documentation & Legalization Checklist</h2>
        <p>To avoid administrative delays or formal rejections, prepare the following verified documentation package:</p>

        <div class="space-y-3 my-6">
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3 p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800">
            <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">✓</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="text-white">Apostilled Criminal Record Check:</strong> Must cover all countries of residence during the previous 2 years, plus a sworn statement of no convictions for the past 5 years. For US citizens, this must be the FBI Identity History Summary apostilled by the US Department of State.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3 p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800">
            <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">✓</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="text-white">Full-Coverage Private Health Insurance:</strong> Must be contracted with an insurer authorized to operate in Spain (e.g., Sanitas, Adeslas, DKV). Policy must have zero copays (<em>sin copago</em>), zero deductibles, and no waiting periods. Travel insurance or basic international nomad policies are routinely rejected.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3 p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800">
            <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">✓</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="text-white">Academic Degree or 3 Years Experience:</strong> Copy of your university bachelor's/master's degree, or documentation evidencing at least 3 years of professional experience in your specific field prior to the application date.
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flex items-start gap-3 p-3 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800">
            <span class="text-emerald-400 font-bold">✓</span>
            <div>
              <strong class="text-white">Sworn Spanish Translations (Traducción Jurada):</strong> All documents not in Spanish (contracts, criminal background checks, certificates of incorporation) must be translated by an official sworn translator certified by the Spanish Ministry of Foreign Affairs (MAEC).
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6 -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">6. Pathway to Permanent Residency & Spanish Citizenship</h2>
        <p>
          Time spent in Spain on a Digital Nomad Visa counts 100% toward <strong>Permanent Residency (Residencia de Larga Duración)</strong> and subsequent Spanish nationality:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
          <li><strong>Years 1 to 3:</strong> Initial in-country permit issued for 3 full years.</li>
          <li><strong>Years 4 to 5:</strong> 2-year renewal upon demonstrating continued economic activity and financial threshold compliance.</li>
          <li><strong>Year 5:</strong> Eligibility to apply for EU Permanent Residency, granting permanent open work and residence rights across the European Union.</li>
          <li><strong>Year 10 (Citizenship):</strong> Standard naturalization timeline for non-EU citizens.</li>
          <li><strong>Year 2 (Accelerated Citizenship):</strong> Citizens of Ibero-American countries (including Mexico, Argentina, Colombia, Brazil, Chile, Peru, etc.), the Philippines, Andorra, and Portugal can apply for Spanish citizenship after just <strong>2 continuous years of legal residence</strong> on the Digital Nomad Visa.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>

    <!-- FAQ Accordion / Box -->
    <section class="mt-16 pt-12 border-t border-slate-800">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions: Spain DNV</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4">
